ets at roughly 92% Republican all point to an easy Republican hold of this open seat. The Democratic nominee has shown modest campaign activity but no evidence of a competitive path.", "watch": "Any late-breaking scandal or a surge of Democratic outside spending could narrow the margin, though nothing in current evidence suggests that.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Ralph Norman (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Ralph Norman's retirement leaves an open seat in a district that voted about eleven points Republican at the presidential level. State Senator Wes Climer cleared the Republican primary unopposed and has raised roughly $884,000, while Democrat Mallory Dittmer has raised about $140,000. Climer has drawn national fundraising help, including an event headlined by Jim Jordan.\n\nEvery major forecaster rates the race Safe or Solid Republican, with only The Economist at Likely Republican, and prediction markets give Democrats roughly a seven percent chance. Dittmer is campaigning with town halls in the Charlotte suburbs, but no polling exists and no outside spending has materialized on her behalf. The seat remains a solid Republican hold in a year with a favorable national environment for the party.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0458, "margin": -10.85}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0227, "margin": -14.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0214, "margin": -14.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0431, "margin": -13.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0389, "margin": -12.0}]}